# HR-23's sanitizer fuzz run: its pins and settings (brief P4-4-HR23-FUZZ;
# docs/phase4/D4-hr23-design.md, section 4.6, "The settings, pinned before
# the run"). Read by fuzz.sh, which refuses to build or run on any file that
# does not match its pin. Zeek's tarball, its signature, the key and option
# (i)'s patch are pinned in ../build.env and checked by `build.sh verify`.
# Every setting here is the same for both builds, C1p and C2, and for the
# three targets.
#
# What the fuzz build is not (D7's N1 on #731). It differs from the
# measured image (../Dockerfile) in more than the sanitizers, coverage and
# static binpac: the compiler is clang 18, not Debian 13's g++; the build is
# debug with -O1, not Release; the system is the runner's Ubuntu 24.04, not
# Debian 13; and its libraries are Ubuntu's versions (the smoke run's
# libssl-dev 3.0.13, where the measured image has libssl3t64 3.5.7). The
# record (fuzz.sh build) names the runner image, clang and every package. So
# the result speaks for Zeek's source and the patches, not for the measured
# binary. A later run that re-checks a report, or a second campaign,
# compares its record's runner image, clang and packages lines with this
# run's and states any difference (D7's condition, adopted by D1).
#
# The harness, the same for both builds (D6's B3 on 9919fd24): the lines
# that add the dnp3_udp target, link the mutator and instrument binpac's
# runtime library (D3's B1 on #731), and the mutator.
HR23_FUZZ_HARNESS_SHA256=9a08488bd66f4ac7d59b9964332e7a9a4a4d34afe60e1a301e0482a30952b8e3
HR23_FUZZ_MUTATOR_SHA256=1074f64da57b24c66bc87297e4fd72402783668d0c1c00a98aea0d1ee9cf6bcf
# The seed conversion and its output: the script, and the corpus it makes
# from Zeek's dnp3_tcp corpus, GT-PCAP's HR-23 cases and malformed set
# (hr23_cases.py) and D4's d4u-* inputs (dispatch_unit.py). The corpus hash
# is the SHA-256 of seeds.py's manifest (one "sha256  target/name" line per
# seed, sorted).
HR23_FUZZ_SEEDS_SHA256=51b75996677b8ded51a81f9cb4104a3dab1c5938c6178bed5af00757b6bc02b8
HR23_FUZZ_CORPUS_SHA256=eb9de3134fb268da9c0055a059a7a613eb38eb78411bfdd3a5be805fc5078e8f
# The compiler, by major version: clang, its libFuzzer and sanitizer
# runtimes, and llvm-profdata and llvm-cov of the same version. The runner
# image's clang-18 packages are not pinned by version; fuzz.sh records
# them, and the runner image's name and version, in the build record.
HR23_FUZZ_CLANG=18
# The sanitizers, as Zeek's own --sanitizers option takes them. Zeek's CMake
# replaces `undefined` by its UB check list, and adds
# -fno-sanitize-recover=<that list>, -fno-omit-frame-pointer and -O1
# (CMakeLists.txt, ZEEK_SANITIZERS). The list is Zeek's own tailored one,
# chosen by ZEEK_TAILORED_UB_CHECKS at configure; fuzz.sh sets it and checks
# that every compile line of binpac's runtime library and of DNP3.cc carries
# these sanitizers and -fno-sanitize-recover=HR23_FUZZ_UB_CHECKS, so that a
# UBSan report stops the run and is counted. Clang 18's whole
# `undefined` group adds `function` and `alignment`, which Zeek's list
# leaves out (its CMakeLists.txt: alignment "TODO: fix associated errors";
# function not supported by GCC). Under the whole group, every campaign
# stopped on a report within its first 3 inputs, a seed, on both builds
# (smoke run 44024284021; what the reports were is not printed, section
# 4.6). A stated limit: those two checks are not run.
HR23_FUZZ_SANITIZERS=address,undefined,fuzzer-no-link
HR23_FUZZ_UB_CHECKS=bool,builtin,bounds,enum,float-cast-overflow,float-divide-by-zero,integer-divide-by-zero,nonnull-attribute,null,pointer-overflow,return,returns-nonnull-attribute,shift,signed-integer-overflow,unreachable,vla-bound,vptr,object-size
# Both builds are instrumented for coverage alike, so the patched build's
# campaign corpus is replayed for coverage on the build that fuzzed it.
HR23_FUZZ_COVERAGE_FLAGS="-fprofile-instr-generate -fcoverage-mapping"
# libFuzzer's options, the same for every campaign and every replay.
# -timeout: seconds per input before a libFuzzer timeout, a report.
# -rss_limit_mb: an out-of-memory stop above it, a report.
# -max_len: octets per input; seeds.py drops a seed's trailing chunks to fit.
# -seed: libFuzzer's random seed.
# -detect_leaks=0: LSan is off. Zeek keeps state across inputs that LSan
#   would report at each input's end; a leak is not counted (a stated limit).
# -report_slow_units: above -timeout, so that no slow-unit file is written
#   (a slow input is not a report).
HR23_FUZZ_TIMEOUT=25
HR23_FUZZ_RSS_LIMIT_MB=4096
HR23_FUZZ_MAX_LEN=8192
HR23_FUZZ_SEED=1
HR23_FUZZ_DETECT_LEAKS=0
HR23_FUZZ_SLOW_UNITS=100000
# Seconds of fuzzing per target per build (D1 6862064732, point 2).
HR23_FUZZ_SECONDS=1800
# The sanitizer runtimes' options. halt_on_error and abort_on_error make
# every report end the process, which libFuzzer counts; detect_leaks=0 as
# above; symbolize=0, since no trace is ever printed or kept beyond the
# runner. detect_odr_violation=1: a global defined twice with different
# sizes is still a report, one defined twice alike is not. Zeek 9.0.1
# bundles SQLite in both libzeek and Broker (sqlite3_version, 7 octets in
# each), and under the default (2) ASan stopped every fuzzer at start-up,
# before any input (smoke run 44017467776). A stated limit.
# Spicy is on, as in the measured image: the default scripts the fuzzers
# load name Spicy's syslog analyzer, so with --disable-spicy every fuzzer
# stopped at start-up on a script error (the same run).
HR23_FUZZ_ASAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:abort_on_error=1:detect_leaks=0:symbolize=0:detect_odr_violation=1
HR23_FUZZ_UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:abort_on_error=1:print_stacktrace=0:symbolize=0
